  "overview": {
    "text": "A magnet is an object that produces a magnetic field—a region where magnetic forces act. Most familiar magnets have north and south poles: opposite poles attract, while like poles repel. Their fields can pull on certain materials, especially iron, cobalt, and nickel; magnets can also influence a nearby compass needle, which turns to align with Earth’s magnetic field. Magnets matter both as simple tools and as part of electromagnetism: a current flowing through a wire coil creates a magnetic field, making an electromagnet. Magnetic compasses have helped people navigate for centuries, using a freely turning magnetized needle to indicate direction.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/magnetism/",
      "https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/compass/",
      "https://www.energy.gov/science/doe-explainsthe-electromagnetic-force"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "text": "Opposite magnetic poles attract; like poles repel.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/magnetism/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Iron, cobalt, and nickel are examples of materials whose atoms can have strong magnetic properties.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/magnetism/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A magnetic compass works because its magnetized needle turns to align with Earth’s magnetic field.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/compass/",
        "https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/magnetism/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Electric current produces a magnetic field; a coil of wire can use this effect to act as an electromagnet.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.energy.gov/science/doe-explainsthe-electromagnetic-force",
        "https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/magnetism/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "People have used magnetic compasses for navigation for hundreds of years.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/magnetism/"
      ]
    }
  ],
